From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: a733: add the RTC timekeeper

The RTC block has been in the tree only as a clock provider, so the kernel
has no /dev/rtc and a board without network boots at whatever date the image
carries. The block's counter is powered from the PMIC's RTCLDO, which
stays up through every PMIC reset except POR, that is DCIN removed or the
power key held > 16 s (AXP318W DS p.22-23, 6.4.5), and the board has no
backup cell (BKUPBAT unconnected).
So the timekeeper keeps time across reboot and poweroff while the supply
stays connected, not across a power cycle. That is the scope of this patch.

== Register set: rtc-sun6i's linear-day layout, unchanged

From the A733 UM V1.00, 9.1.6 register list and 9.1.7 descriptions:

  0x000 LOSC_CTRL_REG      [8] RTC_HHMMSS_ACCE, [7] RTC_DAY_ACCE: set by a
                           write to the time registers, cleared when the
                           write has landed; the time registers may only be
                           written while it reads 0 (9.1.7.1)
  0x010 RTC_DAY_REG        [15:0] day, 0-65535, a linear count (9.1.7.5)
  0x014 RTC_HH_MM_SS_REG   hour [20:16], minute [13:8], second [5:0]
  0x020 ALARM0_DAY_SET     [15:0] day
  0x024 ALARM0_CUR_VLU     hour [20:16], minute [13:8], second [5:0]
  0x028/0x02c/0x030        alarm 0 enable / irq enable / irq pending (W1C)
  0x050 ALARM0_CONFIG_REG  [0] alarm wake-up output
  0x100-0x11c              GP_DATA_REG0-7, kept while VDD_RTC > 0.7 V

That is, field for field, what rtc-sun6i drives when its match data carries
RTC_LINEAR_DAY: SUN6I_RTC_YMD/HMS at 0x10/0x14, the ACC bits 7/8 it polls
before and after each write, the day-plus-HMS alarm at 0x20/0x24, enable,
irq-enable and W1C status at 0x28-0x30, ALARM_CONFIG at 0x50, and a 0x20-byte
nvmem over GP_DATA. The upstream binding lists allwinner,sun60i-a733-rtc with
the fallback allwinner,sun50i-r329-rtc, and in 6.18.36 that fallback selects
RTC_LINEAR_DAY. No driver change.

  reg        0x07090000 + 0x15c: everything below the DCXO control group,
             which `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-rtc-ccu-maps-only-the-dcxo-control-group` gives to rtc_ccu (9.1.6). A523: 0x400, rejected,
             the two nodes would overlap.
  interrupts GIC_SPI 196: RTC_ALARM is INTID 228, vector 0x0390 (UM 12,
             interrupt table).
             A523: SPI 157, rejected.

== Quirks, and what the driver does about them

 - No alarm 1. The UM lists nothing between 0x030 and 0x050. rtc-sun6i's
   probe still writes 0 to 0x044/0x048 and 1 to 0x04c (the alarm-1 enable,
   irq enable and W1C status of older SoCs), as it does on H616/R329.
   Those are reserved offsets here. Both silicon dumps below read 0 there.
 - The UM contradicts itself about alarm 0. 9.1.4.3 describes a counter that
   adds 1 each second and is loaded with "n seconds", which is the older
   sun6i countdown text, and 9.1.5.3 calls the values "count-down". The
   register tables (9.1.7.7, 9.1.7.8) give day plus hour/minute/second
   fields, which is what rtc-sun6i writes: the absolute alarm time. The
   vendor's rtc-sunxi.c writes the absolute time too (type A, "rtc-v201").
   Which one is true is open until an alarm fires on silicon.
 - Epoch. rtc-sun6i counts day 0 = 1970-01-01. The vendor driver counts
   day 1 = 1970-01-01 ("RTC_DAY_REG starts from 1"). A board that boots the
   vendor system and ours in turn reads a one-day offset in each direction.
   The 6 GB board has no vendor system. The 8 GB board does.
 - The clock half stays off: CONFIG_SUN6I_RTC_CCU is kept off in
   the kernel config at this point of the series. With the r329
   fallback, rtc-sun6i's probe calls sun6i_rtc_ccu_probe(), which in 6.18.36
   matches r329 and registers "iosc", "osc32k" and more. Our DT already has
   fixed clocks with those names, so the registration fails and the RTC with
   it, and ccu-sun6i-rtc would take the LOSC mux under a DT that has the
   32k as a fixed clock. The symbol has no other user on this SoC.
   Upstream has since given ccu-sun6i-rtc its own A733 data ("clk: sunxi-ng:
   sun6i-rtc: add a733 support", 2026-07-24, mainline after 6.18). That is
   the one-node shape to migrate to. It is not backported here, because it
   replaces `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-add-minimal-rtc-ccu` and `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-rtc-ccu-add-dcxo` and moves every consumer of rtc_ccu.
 - The counter's 32 kHz source is not touched. The driver writes LOSC_CTRL
   only through its ACC-bit polls, which only read it. The RTC counts
   whatever the firmware left selected. Measured on the 8 GB board, our boot
   chain, 2026-09-17:
   LOSC_CTRL 0x0000401c, LOSC_AUTO_SWT_STA 0. Bit 0 LOSC_SRC_SEL = 0 means
   the 16 MHz RC oscillator as low-frequency clock (9.1.7.1), divided by 32 and by
   INTOSC_CLK_PRESCAL + 1 = 16, so 31.25 kHz nominal, not the 32.768 kHz
   crystal. That runs the counter about 4.6 % slow before any RC tolerance.
   The same word reads 0x0000401d under the vendor stack, with
   LOSC_AUTO_SWT_STA bit 0 = 1, the external crystal, because the vendor
   kernel switches it in ccu-sun60iw2-rtc.c clock_source_init(). The error
   is small over a reboot and large for a clock left running for a day.
   Selecting the crystal is the clock provider's job: the LOSC mux, key
   0x16AA, UM 9.1.5.1. Not this patch.
 - RTC_BGR_REG (PRCM 0x20c, UM 4.2.5.22) resets to 0x0001_0001, gate pass,
   reset de-asserted. Our R-CCU does not model it, so nothing can close it.
   rtc-sun6i's "bus" clock is optional. A523 wires <&r_ccu CLK_BUS_R_RTC>:
   rejected, because that gate does not exist in our R-CCU, and the rtc_ccu
   reads that `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-add-minimal-rtc-ccu` has done since July already depend on it being open.
 - RTC_SPI_CLK_CTRL_REG (0x310, UM 9.1.7.23) gates the bridge every RTC
   register is reached through. Its reset value is 0x13, gated. It reads
   0x80000013 on our chain (same measurement, 2026-09-17), so the firmware opens it. Not
   modelled, the same as for rtc_ccu today.
 - The r329 binding also wants hosc/ahb clocks and #clock-cells. Only
   ccu-sun6i-rtc reads them, and it is off. A #clock-cells on a node that
   provides no clock is a property nobody reads, so the node does not carry
   one and does not satisfy that schema. dtbs_check has not been run on it, and
   6.18.36's binding does not know the A733 compatible anyway.
 - The nvmem exposes GP_DATA_REG0-7, and GP_DATA_REG7 is U-Boot's boot
   counter (U-Boot patch `sunxi-cubie-a7s-bootcount-rtc-gpr`), which
   a7s-boot-good.service clears by
   devmem. The driver keeps no copy, and both sides are single MMIO words.

== What the vendor does and leaves out

The vendor stack has its own RTC driver (rtc-sunxi.c, "allwinner,rtc-v201")
on rtc@7090000, reg 0x320, overlapping its rtc_ccu, and puts the LOSC switch
into its clock driver. That switch polls LOSC_AUTO_SWT_STA with mask 0x10,
but EXT_LOSC_STA is bit 2 and bits 31:3 are reserved (9.1.7.2). The
"crystal is stable" check reads a reserved bit, and bit 0 is switched to the
crystal regardless of its answer. We take neither driver. Mainline's
rtc-sun6i covers the register set as it is.

== Verification

Applies at fuzz 0 on the full series. compile-check and the board dtb build
are green. Not on silicon. What proves it:
  /sys/class/rtc/rtc0/name       "sun6i-rtc 7090000.rtc"; dmesg "registered
                                 as rtc0" and the hctosys line, no -EBUSY
                                 from either node
  /proc/iomem                    07090000-0709015b (rtc) beside
                                 0709015c-0709016f (rtc_ccu, `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-rtc-ccu-maps-only-the-dcxo-control-group`)
  busybox hwclock -w, reboot     RTC and system time agree after a warm
                                 reboot, before the network sets the clock
  echo +60 > .../wakealarm       /proc/interrupts 196 counts once, about 60 s
                                 later. That settles absolute vs countdown.
  0x07090044/48/4c               read 0 after probe
  LOSC_CTRL / STA                read before and after, unchanged by probe
Falsifier: no rtc0, a probe error, or an alarm that fires at the wrong time.

Assisted-by: Claude
---
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dts
@@ -3069,6 +3069,23 @@
 		};
 
 		/*
+		 * RTC time counter, alarm 0 and GP_DATA_REG0-7 (A733 UM 9.1.6),
+		 * the registers below the DCXO control group that rtc_ccu maps.
+		 * Linear 16-bit day counter plus hour/minute/second (9.1.7.5,
+		 * 9.1.7.6): the r329 fallback selects that layout in rtc-sun6i.
+		 * One interrupt, RTC_ALARM = INTID 228 = SPI 196 (UM 12, IRQ
+		 * table). No clocks and no #clock-cells: with SUN6I_RTC_CCU off
+		 * this node provides none, and RTC_BGR_REG (PRCM 0x20c) resets
+		 * to gate pass / reset de-asserted and is not modelled (`arm64-dts-a733-add-the-rtc-timekeeper`).
+		 */
+		rtc: rtc@7090000 {
+			compatible = "allwinner,sun60i-a733-rtc",
+				     "allwinner,sun50i-r329-rtc";
+			reg = <0x0 0x07090000 0x0 0x15c>;
+			interrupts = <GIC_SPI 196 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>;
+		};
+
+		/*
 		 * RTC-CCU (PRCM @0x07090000, AON domain). Provides the two SERDES
 		 * DCXO gates (XO_CTRL1 @0x16c bit4/5); the COMB0 combo-PHY consumes
 		 * dcxo-serdes0. Parent = r-ahb (from the R-CCU). Driver = patch `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-add-minimal-rtc-ccu`.
